  "Number": 45,
  "Question": "If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are two unequal positive numbers, then:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\gt\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt{ab}\\gt\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\gt\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The same inequality will give both comparisons. Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive and unequal, their square roots are unequal. Therefore\n\\[\n(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)^2\n=a+b-2\\sqrt{ab}>0.\n\\]\nThus \\(a+b>2\\sqrt{ab}\\). Dividing by \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b}{2}>\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\nTo obtain the other comparison, multiply \\(a+b>2\\sqrt{ab}\\) by the positive quantity \\(\\sqrt{ab}/(a+b)\\). The inequality direction stays the same, giving\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab}>\\frac{2ab}{a+b}.\n\\]\nHere \\(a+b>0\\), so the denominator cannot be zero. Combining the two strict comparisons gives\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{a+b}{2}>\\sqrt{ab}>\\frac{2ab}{a+b}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice E.",
